    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d374">

                <pb facs="0440" n="422" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg422"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">824.61311/9–646: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Flack</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-09-06T12:00:00-04:00">September 6,
                        1946—noon.</date><lb/>[Received 3:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>876. Minister National Economy<anchor n="41" xml:id="d374-f1"/> yesterday
                    informed Embassy he would submit request thru FonOff for additional 18,000 tons wheat flour, 30,000 tons wheat,
                    delivery at rate of 8,000 tons monthly beginning October. He expressed concern
                    over impending food shortage which he attributes directly to non-arrivals from
                        Argentina.<anchor n="42" xml:id="d374-f2"/> Suplies of beef vegetable oils
                    and dairy products considered dangerously low, all of which normally obtained
                    from Argentina. YPFB oil camps facing food
                    crisis due same cause and have requested Embassy assistance obtain shipment
                    approximately 645 tons miscellaneous foodstuffs.</p>
                <p>Newspaper reports deny existence Argentine embargo but Embassy believes Argentine
                    pressure being exerted for political purposes (possibly related to pending
                    political asylum matter). Further Peruvian Ambassador<anchor n="43"
                        xml:id="d374-f3"/> told me last night that negotiations with his country
                    regarding supplying sugar and petroleum interrupted until asylum matter<anchor
                        n="44" xml:id="d374-f4"/> settled. Bolivian FonOff asked Galbraith<anchor n="45" xml:id="d374-f5"/> orally
                    yesterday that flour and wheat shipments from United States be urgently
                    expedited. Subsecretary<anchor n="46" xml:id="d374-f6"/> also stated orally he
                    had complained to Argentine Chargé<anchor n="47" xml:id="d374-f7"/> yesterday
                    afternoon about non-arrival foodstuffs from Argentina for past 33 days
                    regardless of statements of Argentine high officials. Chargé expressed personal
                    sympathy and said he would inform his Government. Subsecretary also told Chargé
                        FonOff contemplating <pb facs="0441"
                        n="423" xml:id="pg423"/> public statement of food situation caused by
                    Argentine failure to permit exports if no food arrives by September 9.</p>
                <p>Repeated Buenos Aires.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Flack</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d374-f1" n="41" xml:id="d374-f1-content">
                <p>Eduardo Saenz García.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f2" n="42" xml:id="d374-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 887, September 9, 11 a.m., from La Paz, reported that Argentine foods
                    were beginning to enter Bolivia (824.61311/9–946).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f3" n="43" xml:id="d374-f3-content">
                <p>Eduardo Garland Roel.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f4" n="44" xml:id="d374-f4-content">
                <p>For documentation on the revolution in Bolivia, see <ref target="#pg340">pp.
                        340</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f5" n="45" xml:id="d374-f5-content">
                <p>Willard Galbraith, First Secretary of Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f6" n="46" xml:id="d374-f6-content">
                <p>Julio Alvarado.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f7" n="47" xml:id="d374-f7-content">
                <p>Carlos Torres Gigena.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
